---
import Page from "@layouts/Page.astro";
import { LEGAL } from "@lib/legal-env";

const mailto = `mailto:${LEGAL.email}`;
---

<Page
	title="Privacy policy"
	description="What data Factorio Expo holds, why, for how long, who receives it and how to exercise your rights."
>
	<h1>Privacy policy</h1>
	<p>
		<small>Last updated: 9 November 2026</small>
	</p>
	<p>
		Factorio Expo hosts Factorio blueprints. You can upload without an account, and the site
		asks for no email address and no password. This page says what is stored anyway.
	</p>

	<h2>Who is responsible</h2>
	<p>
		The controller is {LEGAL.name}. Contact for anything on this page:{" "}
		<a href={mailto}>{LEGAL.email}</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Age</h2>
	<p>
		You must be at least 16 years old to upload, comment or create an account (see the{" "}
		<a href="/terms-of-use">terms of use</a>). Browsing needs no account.
	</p>

	<h2>What is stored</h2>
	<h3>Without an account</h3>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Uploads.</strong> The exchange string you upload, its decoded contents, the
			title and description you write, tags, the rendered previews and the analysis. An
			anonymous upload has no owner; whoever holds its edit link can change it, so keep the
			link private. Public uploads are visible to everyone and may be copied by others.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Reports.</strong> If you report an entry without being signed in, a keyed hash
			of your IP address (not the address itself) is kept with the report until staff resolve
			it.
		</li>
		<li id="problem-reports">
			<strong>Problem reports</strong> (the bug button, with or without an account). What you
			write, the page you were on and what it showed (the entry and blueprint, game version,
			preview and analysis state, window size and theme), and your browser's name and version
			(its user agent). Signed in, the report is linked to your account; signed out, a keyed
			hash of your IP address (not the address itself) is kept with it until staff resolve it.
			An <strong>email address</strong> only if you give one: it is used for nothing but
			replying to you about that report, and it is deleted when the report is resolved, or 30
			days after you sent it if the report is still open.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Render queue.</strong> Each upload, quick preview or re-render waits in a queue
			for the shared renderer, marked with whose work it is so one person cannot fill it: your
			account, or when you are not signed in a keyed hash of your IP address (not the address
			itself). The mark is deleted as soon as the render is done.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Email.</strong> If you write to the contact address (a question, a takedown
			request, a report of illegal content), your address and message are kept as long as
			needed to handle it.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Page views.</strong> To count one view per visitor per entry per day, a hash of
			your address, browser and the entry is kept in memory only. It is dropped when the daily
			key changes and is never written to the database.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>With an account</h3>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Sign-in methods.</strong> For each linked provider (GitHub, Discord, Google or
			Steam) the provider's name and your id at that provider, and when you linked and last
			used it. For each passkey, its public key, a sign count and dates; the private key never
			leaves your device. A recovery code, shown to you once when you create the account, is
			stored only as a one-way hash: it cannot be read back, and a recovery replaces it. No
			email address and no password is stored, and the site does not request your email from
			any provider.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Username</strong>, which is shown publicly on your uploads and comments, and the
			date you joined.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Profile, if you fill it in.</strong> A status line, your names on the social
			sites you choose to link (YouTube, Twitch, GitHub, Reddit, Bluesky, Steam, X) and which
			of your public entries you pinned. All of it is shown publicly on your profile page.
			Only the name is stored and the link is built from it; the site does not contact those
			services or load anything from them, so a visitor's browser reaches them only by
			following a link.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Sessions.</strong> For each logged-in device: when the session was created, last
			used and when it expires, and when you last signed in.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Content you create.</strong> Entries and their revisions, comments, favorites,
			collections and reports you file.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Moderation.</strong> If staff ban an account, the ban and its reason are
			recorded. To make a ban hold, short-lived IP bans store only keyed hashes of the
			addresses used by banned accounts, never the addresses. When a banned account is
			deleted, keyed hashes of its username and sign-in identities may be kept while the ban
			lasts (a permanent ban: up to 1 year) to refuse sign-ups that evade it.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>Technical data for everyone</h3>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>IP addresses</strong> appear in the web server's access log (see retention
			below) and are held in memory for rate limits, which stop abuse such as mass uploads and
			sign-up spam. Rate limits are not written to disk.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Bot check.</strong> Cloudflare Turnstile checks that you are a person when you
			create an account, and when you upload, preview, add a revision, report an entry or send
			a problem report without being signed in. It runs only on those forms.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Why, and on what legal basis</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Running your account and hosting your uploads</strong>, including the profile
			you choose to fill in (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR, performance of the service you asked for).
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Security and abuse prevention</strong>: logs, rate limits, the render queue's
			per-uploader limit, bans, IP and ban-evasion hashes and the Turnstile check (Art.
			6(1)(f), legitimate interest in keeping a free public site working and safe). You can
			object; see your rights below.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Problem reports</strong>, and the email address you may add to one (Art.
			6(1)(f), legitimate interest in finding and fixing faults in the site, and in replying
			when you ask for it). Adding an email address is optional; you can ask for it, or the
			whole report, to be deleted at any time.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Answering your email and handling notices of illegal content</strong> (Art.
			6(1)(c), the Digital Services Act's duty to handle notices, and Art. 6(1)(f) for
			everything else you write about).
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>The site does not use your data for advertising, profiling or analytics.</p>

	<h2>Who receives data</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Cloudflare, Inc.</strong> delivers the site (CDN and proxy) and runs Turnstile.
			It sees visitors' IP addresses and requests, and may process them in the United States
			under the EU-US Data Privacy Framework or standard contractual clauses.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Hosting provider:</strong> {LEGAL.hostingProvider}. It processes data only on
			the operator's behalf, under a data processing agreement (Art. 28 GDPR).
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Email provider</strong> of the contact address: {LEGAL.emailProvider}, under a
			data processing agreement.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Sign-in providers</strong> (GitHub, Discord, Google, Steam) are independent
			controllers under their own privacy policies. When you choose one, you go to its
			website, and it tells this site only your account id and public name. Nothing is sent to
			them if you use a passkey.
		</li>
		<li>Staff of the site see what is needed for moderation and support.</li>
		<li>
			<strong>GitHub</strong> hosts the site's public issue tracker. To fix a problem you
			reported, staff may copy what you wrote and the page details there, never your email
			address, username or IP hash. Leave personal details out of a problem report.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>Data is not sold and not shared with anyone else.</p>

	<h2 id="cookies">Cookies and browser storage</h2>
	<p>
		Everything below is strictly necessary to provide the service you asked for, so no consent
		banner is shown (Art. 5(3) ePrivacy Directive). The site loads no analytics, advertising,
		embeds or remote fonts.
	</p>
	<div class="table-wrap">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th>Name</th>
					<th>Kind</th>
					<th>Purpose</th>
					<th>Lifetime</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>__Host-expo_session</code>
					</td>
					<td>Cookie, HttpOnly</td>
					<td>Keeps you logged in.</td>
					<td>7 days</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>__Host-expo_oauth</code>
					</td>
					<td>Cookie, HttpOnly</td>
					<td>Protects a sign-in with a provider while you are at the provider.</td>
					<td>10 minutes</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>__Host-expo_signup</code>
					</td>
					<td>Cookie, HttpOnly</td>
					<td>Holds a new provider sign-up until you have chosen a username.</td>
					<td>10 minutes</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>__Host-expo_passkey</code>
					</td>
					<td>Cookie, HttpOnly</td>
					<td>Protects a passkey sign-in, sign-up or confirmation in progress.</td>
					<td>5 minutes</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>theme</code>
					</td>
					<td>Local storage</td>
					<td>Remembers light or dark mode.</td>
					<td>Until you clear it</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>expo:edit-link:*</code>, <code>expo:pending-account-change</code>
					</td>
					<td>Session storage</td>
					<td>
						Hands a new entry's edit link to its page once, and holds a change while you
						confirm it is you.
					</td>
					<td>Until the tab is closed</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>
						<code>__cf_bm</code>, <code>cf_clearance</code>
					</td>
					<td>Cookies set by Cloudflare</td>
					<td>Bot protection of Cloudflare's network and the Turnstile check.</td>
					<td>Set by Cloudflare (30 minutes to a year)</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>

	<h2>How long data is kept</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Access logs:</strong> 14 days. Container logs are capped in size and removed
			with the container.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Sessions:</strong> until they expire after 7 days or you log out; removing a
			sign-in method ends your other sessions.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Deleted accounts:</strong> the account is logged out and its sign-in methods,
			favorites, collections and profile (status, social links, pins) are removed at once, its
			comments become empty tombstones and its entries are hidden and removed from search. The
			rest is erased by a daily clean-up 7 days later. A banned account leaves only keyed
			hashes behind (see ban hashes below).
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Entries:</strong> until deleted by you (or the holder of its edit link), by
			staff, or until the expiry you chose when uploading. Preview images are cached by
			Cloudflare and may stay there for a while after deletion.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Backups:</strong> backups of the database, if made, expire after at most 30
			days; deleted data is gone from them by then and is deleted again if a backup is ever
			restored.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Problem reports:</strong> deleted 7 days after staff resolve them. An email
			address is deleted when the report is resolved, or after 30 days if it is still open.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Render queue marks:</strong> until the render is done, usually minutes.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Ban hashes:</strong> IP-ban hashes last hours; hashes kept for a deleted banned
			account last until its ban ends, a permanent ban up to 1 year.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Your rights</h2>
	<p>Under the GDPR you have the right to:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Access and a copy of your data.</strong> Sign in, open your{" "}
			<a href="/account">account page</a>
			and choose "Export my data". You get one JSON file with your account and profile,
			sign-in methods, sessions, entries with their original exchange strings, comments,
			favorites, pins, collections and reports.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Erasure.</strong> Sign in, open your <a href="/account">account page</a> and
			choose "Delete account". It takes effect at once; the rest is erased 7 days later (see
			deleted accounts above). To delete an anonymous entry, use its edit link.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Rectification.</strong> You can change your entries, comments and profile
			yourself; write to <a href={mailto}>{LEGAL.email}</a> for anything else.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Objection</strong> to processing based on legitimate interest, and restriction
			in the cases the law provides: write to <a href={mailto}>{LEGAL.email}</a>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Complaint</strong> to a data protection supervisory authority, for example in
			the EU country where you live or work.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		A username and a sign-in method are needed to have an account; without them you can still
		browse and upload anonymously.
	</p>

	<h2>Changes</h2>
	<p>
		This page is updated when the site's handling of data changes; the date above shows the
		latest version.
	</p>
</Page>

<style>
	.table-wrap {
		overflow-x: auto;
	}
</style>
